Author: Tristan Mathias <github.gqmij@simplelogin.com>
Date:   Sat Oct 31 08:19:34 2026 -0700

    relation: a query's plain arguments are spelled straight, never through the codec
    
    A reused ask spelled its arguments through the codec on every call — a Writer filled with
    octets, then each octet interpolated and joined with `.` — before it could find its
    cell, so a text-keyed ask cost ~1350 ns against a Map get's ~65. A plain argument is now
    spelled straight: a lone text seat as itself, more seats each as its own text (a text
    by its length and itself, a number as itself) joined by `/`, which stays unambiguous
    because each seat's kind is fixed by the query. Any other kind keeps the codec. `int_arg`
    spells a number the same way, so a lone-int query's interned key and the spelling it
    records agree. The key bound now counts the text itself (key_bound: 5000 bytes, was 20006).
    
    Measured (Sprite, avra-try, 3 runs of 200k reused asks inside one query): a text key
    1329–1396 -> 186–220 ns/ask; an int key 82–113 ns (unchanged); a Map get 62–89 ns. A
    text ask is now one Map get (the key's interning) plus an int-keyed ask. The cli, cleared
    unshared store, 5 runs, medians: warm 89 -> 92 ms, comment edit 179 -> 186 ms (noise);
    with G1 on top 100 / 184 ms. std-relation 299/299 + 31 programs; compiler/tests 1687/1687.
    
    Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
    Claude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01QBqJuoi8EUrw37A8m4Qfto

